<header class="page-hero">
  <h1>Try it. It is the real firmware.</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-subtitle">
    The device&rsquo;s code runs in your browser, as is.<br>
    Switch it on, add traffic, press the keys.
  </p>
</header>

<section id="simulator">
  <%= render "shared/simulator",
        wake: "Hold the lower key to switch it back on.",
        traffic: "Put an aircraft in the sky",
        keys: "Once it is on, the keyboard flies it while it is on screen. The arrows work like a stick: down to climb, up to descend.",
        on: "running",
        off: "off",
        menu: "menu",
        glass: "The simulated e-paper panel of a skyBlip Go",
        key: "The device button: press to open the menu of the page you are on, hold two seconds to switch off, hold it again to switch on",
        pad: "The capacitive pad: tap to change page, hold one second to come back to the radar",
        reset: "RST: reboot the device",
        caption: "Sensors are simulated, the firmware is not. It is the build this site was published from.",
        missing: "The simulator is not part of this build of the site." %>

  <p class="note">
    The sky is empty until you ask for something. Every aircraft you add
    transmits real ADS-L frames, which the firmware receives, decodes and plots
    exactly as it would in flight. The lamp on the left is the firmware&rsquo;s:
    it lights when traffic sets off the alarm. The one on the right belongs to
    the charger, so it stays dark here. Every capture on these pages comes off this same
    firmware.
  </p>
</section>

